# Component Testing Rules

## Testing Library Queries

Priority order (highest to lowest):
1. `getByRole` — accessible semantics (buttons, headings, links)
2. `getByLabelText` — form inputs
3. `getByTestId` — non-semantic containers, complex widgets
4. `getByText` / `getByPlaceholderText` — content-based (brittle with i18n)

## User Interactions

- ALWAYS use `@testing-library/user-event` for interactions
- NEVER use `fireEvent` — it doesn't simulate real browser events
- Create user instance once per test: `const user = userEvent.setup()`

```typescript
// GOOD
const user = userEvent.setup();
await user.type(screen.getByLabelText('Name'), 'Alice');
await user.click(screen.getByRole('button'));

// BAD
fireEvent.change(screen.getByLabelText('Name'), { target: { value: 'Alice' } });
fireEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button'));
```

## Provider Wrapping

- Create a single `test-utils.tsx` with all providers
- Re-export `render` from test-utils, not from @testing-library/react
- Include: MemoryRouter (if routing), context providers, i18n, theme

## What NOT to Test

- Internal state of custom hooks (test the component that uses them)
- CSS classes or inline styles
- Private methods or implementation details
- Third-party library internals
